  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the Mourillon beaches site, the City of Toulon has set up a dog park dedicated to dogs, with two clearly separate areas, one for small animals, the other for the largest dogs. The municipal order places them between the car park and the beaches; the tourist office listing says "Lido beach side" and "small and large dogs in 2 different, adjoining areas". The site covers more than 40 hectares, with a large wooded park and a car park of more than 1,300 free spaces (City of Toulon).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The City of Toulon gives no details on the dog park's hours or maximum length of stay: ask on-site. Its leaflet "Toulon, ville amie des chiens" states that the leash is mandatory throughout the municipality, that dog areas let dogs move freely in some green spaces, and that picking up droppings is mandatory everywhere, including inside dog parks (an unpicked dropping can bring a class 4 fine of 135 euros).</p>
      <p>On the rest of the Mourillon beaches, the municipal order of 28 September 2026 allows dogs on a leash all year round, Henri-Fabre promenade included, but not on the sand, the lawns, the play areas or the sports facilities; category 1 and 2 dogs must also be muzzled. This order repeals the orders of 26 June 2025 and 12 May 2026: some older pages still quote the former summer rule (promenade only between 6am and 8.30am).</p>
      <p>This dog park is separate from the dog beach at Anse Tabarly: the order provides a swimming area dedicated to dogs all year round on the peninsula, with a pontoon to help dogs get into the water. The dog park itself is a land-based space.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Mourillon area has two distinct canine spaces, the dog park for running and the Anse Tabarly dog beach for swimming; check which one matches what you're looking for.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The four sandy coves of the Mourillon beaches (Lido, Mistral, La Source and Les Pins) and Anse Tabarly, devoted to water sports, are nearby; dogs are not allowed on the sand of these coves.</p>
